 forms of I'll call it irrational  behavior  there's the form that says even although  the number of cases and hospitalizations  is going are going up in my state I'm  still going to go back to work and I'm  gonna go back to the stores and I'm not  gonna wear a mask because the bells of  he'll go tingling for you but not for me  in other words people essentially don't  think they personally are going to get  the virus now that may be because  they're young and they say to themselves  well I've read that it really only kills  the elderly and therefore my risk is  pretty low but what's odd is that we  find quite a lot of older people who say  in surveys that they're in favor of  rapid reopening even before the  pandemics under control in in their area  on the side of people who recently  protested it might be said that outdoors  it's much harder to catch covered 19  than indoors and the most protests are  outdoors therefore there's not much risk  but when you look at the crowds they're  pretty densely packed and the shouting  and we know from the literature that the  key to transmission of SARS cough to is  it's not just talking in a normal way  it's shouting and singing that really  along with coughing and sneezing that  propels the virus from a carrier to  others so there's some risk involved in  going to these protests most of the  protesters are relatively young but I  saw Mitt Romney out in the streets  yesterday wearing a mask prudently but  it's hard to tell I've just looked at  many shots and video clips I wouldn't  say a majority of protesting people have  been wearing masks and I wouldn't say  that they're all as it were young enough  not to worry in any case even if you are  young enough not to be too worried about  SARS cough to you you should be worrying  about the older people that you're going  to meet subsequent to your protest had a  conversation my daughter about this last  night she's distinctly to the left of me  but it eclis but she had wisely resisted  peer group pressure to go on a march in  London on the ground that she would soon  be seeing her mother and grandmother who  are in the vulnerable age group  so that that seemed like a pretty good  example of somebody coming to a good  decision even under peer group pressure  and peer group pressures a huge part of  what's going on here  people may suspect they're doing  something that's not prudent but they  kind of give in to the peer group  pressure I was glad what my daughter  resisted that when it comes to beliefs  about blame and responsibility around  the virus there's definitely some  interesting network effects going on I  was interested to see in your recent  interview with Andy serwer I'll let you  sort of characterize in detail your  position but at least generally speaking  you seem to probably assign less  responsibility to the federal government  and Trump's actions than I would  although I do believe that there have  been unfair accusations also cast at the  federal government which were either  beyond anyone's control or more relevant  to a mayor or a governor for example  what do you think are the unfair  criticisms that are or blame that is  being placed on the president maybe we  can start there  well I think it's worth saying right  away that the primary responsibility for  this pandemic lies with the Chinese  government and also the provincial  authorities in Hebei province who really  criminally fail to deal with the problem  and were slow to admit the extent to  which human to human transmission was  happening a granade imposing travel bans  until it was really much too late so  that's part one part two of the answer  is that the media obsession with  President Trump which he of course  encourages lead I think to a tendency to  overemphasize the president's role in  reality in a crisis of this sort the  agencies that really matter are those  responsible for pandemic preparedness  and that is a part of the Department of  Health and Human Services not just CDC  which has got a lot of the stake but we  have an undersecretary or I think an  Assistant Secretary to be exact at DHHS  whose job this is and who had written  and spoken a good deal on the subject in  fact if you delve into it the federal  bureaucracy had generated any number of  reports on pandemic preparedness and  PowerPoint decks with multiple slides  and indeed on paper the United States  was well prepared for a situation like  this it was just that when it actually  happened back in January and right  through February and into March that the  agencies responsible the people whose  job this was did terribly and we can say  that I think fairly because we can see  where it was done much better in  countries where public health  bureaucracies were quick to respond like  in for example Taiwan or South Korea or  Germany for that matter you don't just  need to look at Asian countries so the  u.s. in common with I'd say also the  United Kingdom did really badly at  dealing with a problem they were  supposed to be ready for making it all  about Trump I think is a mistake which  misunderstands the nature of government  it is not that the president sits there  in the White House with his phone ready  to tweet deciding on everything that's  just not the way it works the federal  government is a hugely complex thing  with multiple agencies more than 60 in  total and there is an agency who has  this job and they failed and not nearly  enough has been written about that in my  view this is not to say Trump did great  he did quite badly his decision-making  was erratic he had some good instincts  like we have to do something about  people coming in from China but it came  much too late to make any difference at  all at the end of January and I think  instinctively Trump felt that lockdowns  were a mistake which i think was right  too because it was an incredibly blunt  instrument to deal with the contagion  and not necessary if in fact you've done  the right things early on so you know  you you can't say it's all his fault  I think that's a great mistake but nor  can you say he's done a great job I mean  in the end the the buck stops with the  president the United States as Harry  Truman said long ago and so one can't  say well it was all the faults of dhhs  not the president's fault he's the  president ultimately the buck stops with  him there's no question that the United  States has handled this pandemic very  much worse than  should have very much worse than other  comparable countries and that is why we  have the highest death toll and may well  end up with a very high excess mortality  rate by the end of the year so maybe  we're not actually that far I mean in  hearing you lay it out it seems like  some of this is about the president as  the per you know HHS run by Alex a czar  who early on thought it was a good idea  to port a former Labradoodle dealer in  charge of the day-to-day okay that was a  bad decision  he is Trump's pick to run HHS so a lot  of this seems to be the semantics of  Trump's delegation versus Trump's  actions I mean I think the biggest  critique certainly would be when you  look at South Korea and the u.s. finding  our first cases within hours of each  other  South Korea rapid test development fever  clinics isolation contact tracing that  happened six weeks later or it started  to happen in a serious way six weeks  later the testing capacity was a joke  I do think you know you can't blame  Trump for a hundred thousand one hundred  and ten thousand deaths of course there  was probably a period there I would say  around you know aggressively March tenth  less aggressively March twenty-fifth  were the requests for a coordinated  50-state stay at home that would be long  over by now had it been done from the  start and we wouldn't have what we now  have rolling opening and closing Florida  and Texas spiking well fortunately here  in Massachusetts were doing well that  certainly was a lost opportunity for  Trump and I would even say it was a lost  opportunity from a campaign standpoint I  think that he has now suffered greatly  because he didn't do some of these  things that may have been economically  unpopular at first but would likely have  him with a much higher than 38% approval  today I wonder if you agree it's  difficult on that I mean my sense is  that by mid-march it was it was too late  and that blanket shelter-in-place orders  would not really have had a huge impact  by that stage other than in fact to  magnify the  Shok let me reiterate the key decision  points were in January as soon as it  became clear there was a problem yeah  and right into February when President  Trump and many others in the  administration were downplaying the  threat and not only president Trump  remember at the end of January when  Trump called for a travel ban on Chinese  passengers coming into the US he was  widely criticized in the liberal media  accused essentially of racism and there  were multiple articles at that time in  liberal circles saying it's just the flu  in fact you should worry much more about  the flu and for me as somebody who was  writing no this really isn't the flu  this is very much more serious it was  maddening to see such ill-informed  rubbish being published so widely there  was a bipartisan failure to recognize  the scale of the threat that went on  right into February I can remember being  in Washington in mid-february trying to  convince people in the administration  this thing is real you have got to do  something and you particularly have to  tell the president to stop saying there  is no threat to the United States it's  just gonna disappear so it was it was  then that I think the really disastrous  mistakes were made by mid-march they had  been insouciant for so long that they  were actually thrown into panic by  another Neil Ferguson it's worth  clarifying that I'm not him any il  Ferguson the epidemiologist at Imperial  College London who published a report on  March 16th saying that if you didn't  have lockdowns 2.2 million people were  going to die in the United States and  half a million in the UK and both the  London and Washington governments  flipped when they saw this as did many  people who saw the report or read about  it and we went from total insouciance to  total panic this was really the worst of  both worlds because we had dithered  around when we should have been taking  early action and then we did a bunch of  extremely blunt instrument measures like  shelters and place orders and economic  lock downs that it doesn't seem to me  have radically changed the spread or  slowed the spread of the virus social  distancing was something that mattered a  lot when people adopted that even not at  the order of the state it had a big  impact the lock downs have clearly done  much more  harm than public health good so as I  said we've ended up with this kind of  worst of both worlds and it's not over  and for me the depressing thing is  seeing people behaving all over the  country as if it is over in in defiance  of the reality that if you just look at  numbers of confirmed cases there are  second waves already in about a dozen  states it's already happening and let me  ask ya so the last thing I wanted to  touch on was for a while I've been  fearing that there was going to be a  point at which it's going to be sort of  unclear what each person should be doing  here in Massachusetts today outdoor  dining gets to open and then this is  phase 2a and then we're going to get to  phase 2b where something else can happen  and you know I don't know I'm in my  mid-30s I'm probably fine to go and sit  outside at a restaurant but should I do  it and is it if you need a mask is this  is that a signal that I shouldn't be  doing it or that what do you make of  this phase now that could last really it  could last until there's a vaccine I  don't know how long it's going to last  where there's going to be a lot of  needing to sort of figure out for  oneself what is the appropriate behavior  yes I think you're right to feel  confused and uncertain because mixed  policy signals at the national and state  level have created exactly that sense of  confusion all over the country and this  goes back to what we were talking about  earlier when people are uncertain but  get their information from let's say  more sensationalistic sources then they  will take action that is in fact not  going to help and might even harm them  hydroxychloroquine for example is an  example of something that was touted as  a remedy not just by the president but  by many other people but the only really  good scientific research on theirs  suggests that it has no real  prophylactic effect and it's quite  harmful if you are suffering seriously  from covert 19 so as people try to work  out what to do they're battling against  a combination of poor quality public  information from the government I mean I  think our public health bureaucracy has  failed at  Communications level by the way really  interesting piece on this subject in the  New York Times just about a week ago  going through what exactly went wrong at  CDC it's well worth reading unlike many  things in the New York Times and and it  actually shows a classic pathology of a  burek bureaucratic organization very  hierarchical very slow-moving and easily  bullied by the White House when the  going got tough it is a very very stark  illustration of a problem that the great  physicist Richard Feynman identified  long ago in the case of the Challenger  Space Shuttle disaster  it's the mid-level bureaucracy that's  often the problem in disasters like this  so we as individuals are getting poor  information from the public authorities  and even worse information from the  internet what are we going to do  well we have to make sure that we read  carefully what is out there that is  reputable  that is to say has some solid basis in  scientific research and on that basis my  advice to you is you probably fine to go  and eat outside in Massachusetts on a  nice day you should wear a mask and you  should really keep your distance from  from other people as far as you can just  to be on the safe side because  Massachusetts is a state where there are  still quite a high level of cases of  code 19 being diagnosed that everyday  would I advise you to go into a crowded  office or to take a crowd form of public  transport no I'd say avoid those things  avoid any crowded indoor space  especially if people are shouting or  singing so forget choir practice and if  somebody says let's have a reopening  party come on round to my place up  lightly decline because those are very  likely that super spreader events that  we're going to get in the coming weeks  and months that will keep this pandemic  going in the United States for much  longer than was necessary seems  eminently reasonable I would say we've  been speaking with Neil Ferguson he is a  historian and the new PBS series net  world explores the history and science  of networks Neil really appreciate your  time today  thanks David it's beautiful  enjoy that enjoy that dinner that's the  problem that's the that's the logistical  issue I will figure it out thank you  thanks
 

